{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WvEddMuaY13uEbJsu2mhjq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — exec-sim-repair-verifier\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25, 2026)\n5\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n6\t- Extended fallback: ICSE 2027 (Jun 23 abstract, Jun 30 paper)\n7\t- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (14 days from project start)\n8\t\n9\t## Core Claims (3 conditions)\n10\t\n11\t1. **Complexity-Dependent Reformulation Advantage**: Binary pass/fail classification and execution output generation (CWM-style) achieve equivalent accuracy on simple function-level code (4B: F1 diff 0.9pp), but classification scales better with model size (8B 3-seed mean accuracy gap=2.43pp; seed42: 2.81pp, cls 90.48% vs cwm 87.67%) and to repo-level complexity where CWM failure modes—token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction errors—dominate. Zero-shot LLM-as-judge baselines (8B: 64.4%, 32B NF4: 71.7%) are far below fine-tuned 4B (88.4%), confirming fine-tuning necessity. (Validated function-level near-null + 8B scaling evidence; repo-level SWE-bench experiments in progress. Fallback: if repo-level also null, reframe to efficiency-equivalent accuracy—same F1 with 1-token vs N-token inference.)\n12\t\n13\t2. **Diagnostic Granularity At Equivalent Accuracy**: Per-test classification achieves equivalent variant-level accuracy to trajectory-level scoring (TOST equivalence test p=0.007, ±2pp margin; 5-seed pooled gap -0.40pp, 95% CI [-1.32, +0.52]) while providing actionable diagnostic information (which specific tests a patch fails). The per-test formulation gains diagnostic granularity without sacrificing accuracy—potentially enabling targeted re-repair and oracle-based test selection. (Evidence: 5-paired clustered bootstrap CI, 114 clusters, 10000 resamples. Paper must justify ±2pp margin: in code verification, 2pp variant-level difference is negligible given ICC=0.191/DEFF=3.97. Seeds 789/0 used 4-bit quantization — note in paper. Downstream re-repair experiment still needed for practical impact claim.)\n14\t\n15\t3. **Accuracy-Utility Frontier Characterization**: The effectiveness of per-test verification depends critically on test distribution characteristics. We empirically show: (a) per-test advantage concentrates in instances with multiple FAIL_TO_PASS tests (31% of SWE-bench Verified); (b) PASS_TO_PASS regression detection provides signal only when regression rate exceeds ~10%; (c) block-correlated prediction errors amplify per-test advantage through reduced effective degrees of freedom. These findings provide a practical decision framework for when per-test simulation is worth deploying.\n16\t\n17\t## Story Spine\n18\t\n19\t**Problem**: Test-time scaling for code repair requires ranking candidate patches, but verification paradigms face a granularity-cost tradeoff. Full execution costs ~$1-3 and minutes per candidate. Execution-free alternatives like SWE-RM predict trajectory-level binary correctness at low cost but provide only coarse signals that cannot distinguish between candidates failing on different test subsets.\n20\t\n21\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Code World Models (Self-Execution Simulation, CWM) predict execution outputs but suffer from token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction failures. SWE-RM achieves strong trajectory-level scoring but loses per-test diagnostic information. No work studies per-test binary classification as a dedicated verification formulation, or compares classification vs generation formulations for code execution simulation at controlled model sizes.\n22\t\n23\t**Our framing**: We reformulate execution simulation from an open-ended generation task (predict execution trace/output) to a closed-form classification task (predict binary pass/fail per test). On simple function-level code, both formulations achieve equivalent accuracy (~87% F1), because short execution outputs do not trigger CWM failure modes. However, on repo-level code with long execution traces and complex outputs, classification sidesteps token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction errors, yielding a complexity-dependent advantage. We empirically ground this through (1) controlled function-level experiments establishing equivalence, (2) repo-level experiments demonstrating the complexity threshold, (3) oracle accuracy-utility frontier analysis revealing when per-test granularity helps, and (4) error complementarity analysis showing CLS/CWM have systematically opposite failure modes (CLS: miss bugs 75.5%, CWM: false alarms 83.1%), with oracle ensemble reaching 90.63%. (Downstream re-repair experiment is stretch goal; if not completed, frame as future work.)\n24\t\n25\t**Headline result template**: \"On function-level benchmarks, classification and generation verifiers achieve equivalent F1 (~87%), but classification scales better with model size (8B: +2.43pp accuracy) and on repo-level SWE-bench tasks outperforms by [Z] percentage points as CWM failure modes emerge. CLS and CWM exhibit complementary error patterns (oracle ensemble: 90.63%), suggesting formulation diversity as a verification strategy.\"\n26\t\n27\t## Venue Fit Risks\n28\t\n29\t1. **EMNLP vs SE venue**: Core concern is code repair verification, which may be perceived as SE rather than NLP. Mitigation: frame as (a) probing LLM code semantic reasoning capabilities through per-test prediction, and (b) a general NLP methodology insight about classification vs generation formulations applicable beyond code.\n30\t\n31\t2. **Negative P2P finding**: P2P regression rate ~1% means per-test is not universally better. Must present honestly as a characterization finding, not bury it. This actually strengthens the paper by showing nuanced understanding rather than overclaiming.\n32\t\n33\t3. **Function-level to repo-level gap**: Function-level results show classification ≈ generation. Repo-level extension is now CRITICAL (not optional) to demonstrate the complexity-dependent advantage. Requires SWE-bench Docker infrastructure. If repo-level also shows no advantage, paper pivots to efficiency framing (same accuracy, lower inference cost) + oracle characterization.\n34\t\n35\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Mitigations\n36\t\n37\t| Concern | Mitigation Experiment |\n38\t|---------|----------------------|\n39\t| \"Classification is trivially easier than generation -- not interesting\" | Function-level null result proves classification is NOT trivially easier; advantage is complexity-dependent, demonstrated by repo-level gap |\n40\t| \"Per-test is just trajectory-level with extra steps when T=1\" | Oracle frontier analysis shows T distribution sensitivity; honest reporting of T=1 equivalence |\n41\t| \"No practical downstream impact\" | Diagnostic re-repair experiment shows resolve rate improvement |\n42\t| \"Small-scale function-level results don't generalize\" | Repo-level SWE-bench extension (if resources allow) |\n43\t| \"CWM comparison is unfair (different output format)\" | Matched model, data, compute; discuss target asymmetry limitation |\n44\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d905b624-c5ef-406f-b658-55e6d1ececb7","uuid":"1168fd7d-0f42-47e6-8094-5faec76ef5de","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:36:38.383Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T150201/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — exec-sim-repair-verifier\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25, 2026)\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Extended fallback: ICSE 2027 (Jun 23 abstract, Jun 30 paper)\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (14 days from project start)\n\n## Core Claims (3 conditions)\n\n1. **Complexity-Dependent Reformulation Advantage**: Binary pass/fail classification and execution output generation (CWM-style) achieve equivalent accuracy on simple function-level code (4B: F1 diff 0.9pp), but classification scales better with model size (8B 3-seed mean accuracy gap=2.43pp; seed42: 2.81pp, cls 90.48% vs cwm 87.67%) and to repo-level complexity where CWM failure modes—token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction errors—dominate. Zero-shot LLM-as-judge baselines (8B: 64.4%, 32B NF4: 71.7%) are far below fine-tuned 4B (88.4%), confirming fine-tuning necessity. (Validated function-level near-null + 8B scaling evidence; repo-level SWE-bench experiments in progress. Fallback: if repo-level also null, reframe to efficiency-equivalent accuracy—same F1 with 1-token vs N-token inference.)\n\n2. **Diagnostic Granularity At Equivalent Accuracy**: Per-test classification achieves equivalent variant-level accuracy to trajectory-level scoring (TOST equivalence test p=0.007, ±2pp margin; 5-seed pooled gap -0.40pp, 95% CI [-1.32, +0.52]) while providing actionable diagnostic information (which specific tests a patch fails). The per-test formulation gains diagnostic granularity without sacrificing accuracy—potentially enabling targeted re-repair and oracle-based test selection. (Evidence: 5-paired clustered bootstrap CI, 114 clusters, 10000 resamples. Paper must justify ±2pp margin: in code verification, 2pp variant-level difference is negligible given ICC=0.191/DEFF=3.97. Seeds 789/0 used 4-bit quantization — note in paper. Downstream re-repair experiment still needed for practical impact claim.)\n\n3. **Accuracy-Utility Frontier Characterization**: The effectiveness of per-test verification depends critically on test distribution characteristics. We empirically show: (a) per-test advantage concentrates in instances with multiple FAIL_TO_PASS tests (31% of SWE-bench Verified); (b) PASS_TO_PASS regression detection provides signal only when regression rate exceeds ~10%; (c) block-correlated prediction errors amplify per-test advantage through reduced effective degrees of freedom. These findings provide a practical decision framework for when per-test simulation is worth deploying.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: Test-time scaling for code repair requires ranking candidate patches, but verification paradigms face a granularity-cost tradeoff. Full execution costs ~$1-3 and minutes per candidate. Execution-free alternatives like SWE-RM predict trajectory-level binary correctness at low cost but provide only coarse signals that cannot distinguish between candidates failing on different test subsets.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Code World Models (Self-Execution Simulation, CWM) predict execution outputs but suffer from token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction failures. SWE-RM achieves strong trajectory-level scoring but loses per-test diagnostic information. No work studies per-test binary classification as a dedicated verification formulation, or compares classification vs generation formulations for code execution simulation at controlled model sizes.\n\n**Our framing**: We reformulate execution simulation from an open-ended generation task (predict execution trace/output) to a closed-form classification task (predict binary pass/fail per test). On simple function-level code, both formulations achieve equivalent accuracy (~87% F1), because short execution outputs do not trigger CWM failure modes. However, on repo-level code with long execution traces and complex outputs, classification sidesteps token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction errors, yielding a complexity-dependent advantage. We empirically ground this through (1) controlled function-level experiments establishing equivalence, (2) repo-level experiments demonstrating the complexity threshold, (3) oracle accuracy-utility frontier analysis revealing when per-test granularity helps, and (4) error complementarity analysis showing CLS/CWM have systematically opposite failure modes (CLS: miss bugs 75.5%, CWM: false alarms 83.1%), with oracle ensemble reaching 90.63%. (Downstream re-repair experiment is stretch goal; if not completed, frame as future work.)\n\n**Headline result template**: \"On function-level benchmarks, classification and generation verifiers achieve equivalent F1 (~87%), but classification scales better with model size (8B: +2.43pp accuracy) and on repo-level SWE-bench tasks outperforms by [Z] percentage points as CWM failure modes emerge. CLS and CWM exhibit complementary error patterns (oracle ensemble: 90.63%), suggesting formulation diversity as a verification strategy.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n1. **EMNLP vs SE venue**: Core concern is code repair verification, which may be perceived as SE rather than NLP. Mitigation: frame as (a) probing LLM code semantic reasoning capabilities through per-test prediction, and (b) a general NLP methodology insight about classification vs generation formulations applicable beyond code.\n\n2. **Negative P2P finding**: P2P regression rate ~1% means per-test is not universally better. Must present honestly as a characterization finding, not bury it. This actually strengthens the paper by showing nuanced understanding rather than overclaiming.\n\n3. **Function-level to repo-level gap**: Function-level results show classification ≈ generation. Repo-level extension is now CRITICAL (not optional) to demonstrate the complexity-dependent advantage. Requires SWE-bench Docker infrastructure. If repo-level also shows no advantage, paper pivots to efficiency framing (same accuracy, lower inference cost) + oracle characterization.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Mitigations\n\n| Concern | Mitigation Experiment |\n|---------|----------------------|\n| \"Classification is trivially easier than generation -- not interesting\" | Function-level null result proves classification is NOT trivially easier; advantage is complexity-dependent, demonstrated by repo-level gap |\n| \"Per-test is just trajectory-level with extra steps when T=1\" | Oracle frontier analysis shows T distribution sensitivity; honest reporting of T=1 equivalence |\n| \"No practical downstream impact\" | Diagnostic re-repair experiment shows resolve rate improvement |\n| \"Small-scale function-level results don't generalize\" | Repo-level SWE-bench extension (if resources allow) |\n| \"CWM comparison is unfair (different output format)\" | Matched model, data, compute; discuss target asymmetry limitation |\n","numLines":44,"startLine":1,"totalLines":44}}}
